      "text": "To determine the value of \\(a\\) so that the given lines are perpendicular, we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. **Find the slope of the first line:**\n   The first line is given by the equation:\n   \\[\n   y = 2x + 13\n   \\]\n   This is in the slope-intercept form \\(y = mx + b\\), where \\(m\\) is the slope. Therefore, the slope of the first line is \\(m_1 = 2\\).\n\n2. **Determine the slope of the second line:**\n   The second line is given by the equation:\n   \\[\n   6y + ax = 6\n   \\]\n   First, we need to rewrite this equation in the slope-intercept form \\(y = mx + b\\). Start by isolating \\(y\\):\n   \\[\n   6y = -ax + 6\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   y = -\\frac{a}{6}x + 1\n   \\]\n   From this equation, we can see that the slope of the second line is \\(m_2 = -\\frac{a}{6}\\).\n\n3. **Use the condition for perpendicular lines:**\n   Two lines are perpendicular if the product of their slopes is \\(-1\\). Therefore, we set up the equation:\n   \\[\n   m_1 \\cdot m_2 = -1\n   \\]\n   Substituting the slopes \\(m_1 = 2\\) and \\(m_2 = -\\frac{a}{6}\\) into the equation, we get:\n   \\[\n   2 \\cdot \\left(-\\frac{a}{6}\\right) = -1\n   \\]\n   Simplify the left side:\n   \\[\n   -\\frac{2a}{6} = -1\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   -\\frac{a}{3} = -1\n   \\]\n   Multiply both sides by \\(-3\\) to solve for \\(a\\):\n   \\[\n   a = 3\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the value of \\(a\\) that makes the lines perpendicular is \\(\\boxed{3}\\).",
