      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To determine the value of \\( a \\) for which the given lines are perpendicular, we need to use the property that the slopes of two perpendicular lines are negative reciprocals of each other.\n\nLet's start by identifying the slopes of both lines. The first equation is already in slope-intercept form:\n\\[ y = 2x + 13. \\]\nFrom this, we can see that the slope \\( m_1 \\) of the first line is \\( 2 \\).\n\nNext, we need to rewrite the second equation in slope-intercept form:\n\\[ 6y + ax = 6. \\]\nWe'll isolate \\( y \\) on one side of the equation:\n\\[ 6y = -ax + 6. \\]\nDividing through by 6, we get:\n\\[ y = -\\frac{a}{6}x + 1. \\]\nFrom this, we can see that the slope \\( m_2 \\) of the second line is \\( -\\frac{a}{6} \\).\n\nFor the lines to be perpendicular, the product of their slopes must be \\( -1 \\):\n\\[ m_1 \\cdot m_2 = -1. \\]\nSubstituting the slopes we found:\n\\[ 2 \\cdot \\left( -\\frac{a}{6} \\right) = -1. \\]\nSimplifying the left side:\n\\[ -\\frac{2a}{6} = -1, \\]\n\\[ -\\frac{a}{3} = -1. \\]\nMultiplying both sides by \\(-3\\) to solve for \\( a \\):\n\\[ a = 3. \\]\n\nThus, the value of \\( a \\) that makes the lines perpendicular is \\(\\boxed{3}\\).",
